,"type":"genre","name":"Balladry","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":3,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":9343125461845,"type":"genre","name":"Adult Contemporary","n":3}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":154312107829352,"name":"El soul se vuelve funky","book":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","start":1968,"end":1975,"desc":"Tras la Bomba de James Brown, el soul dio un giro radical: el acento en el primer tiempo se oyó en «Superstition» de Stevie Wonder, «Inner City Blues» de Marvin Gaye y «Cloud Nine» de los Temptations; lo que empezó como un efecto se volvió norma, y el jam funk pasó a ser imprescindible para que un artista negro llegara a la gente en los setenta. Marvin Gaye preguntó «What’s Goin’ On».","color":"#facc15","family":"Soul y proto-funk: Stax, Nueva Orleans, Motown","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/#soul-funky"},{"id":195472159040058,"name":"Commodores","book":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","start":1968,"end":1982,"desc":"Uno de los pocos intentos de Motown con bandas autosuficientes: los Mighty Mystics del Instituto Tuskegee, con Lionel Richie, la guitarra de Thomas McClary y el clavinet de Milan Williams, de gira mundial abriendo para los Jackson 5. Machine Gun (1974), sin una balada, era funk de corral sureño con trucos futuristas; con cada disco Richie cantaba más baladas. Hot on the Tracks (1976) y Commodores (1977), con «Easy», «Zoom» y «Brick House», uno de los pocos temas funk que superaron la fórmula del disco. Con «Three Times a Lady» fueron la primera de muchas bandas que abandonaron el funk por la fama pop: quizá su funk era solo una moda, cosa poco honrosa para una música nacida de la Revolución Negra.","color":"#22c55e","family":"Bandas de funk de los setenta","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/#commodores"}],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Lead Singer and Songwriter for Commodores","body":"Lionel Richie was the lead singer on \"Slippery When Wet\" and a songwriter for the Commodores. \"The lead singer on Slippery When Wet was also a songwriter. You've probably heard of him, Lionel Richie...\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f2ad1ba84fb8f045799"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Motown Discovery","body":"Lionel Richie was among the important black artists discovered by Motown Records, a label instrumental in shaping music from the 1960s and 1970s. \"descubrió la nera de la niña del Richie.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"We are the world","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XTJKpU0lhls"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Multi-Platinum 80s Solo Career","body":"After scoring a couple more up-tempo hits with the Commodores, Lionel Richie departed the band to embark on a multi-platinum solo career in the 1980s. \"before he left for a multi-platinum 80s solo career.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f2ad1ba84fb8f045799"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Tendency Towards Softer Compositions","body":"While a member of the Commodores, Lionel Richie gravitated towards composing softer, more sentimental songs, which ultimately became the band's \"secret sentimental weapon\" for pop crossover. \"Lionel tended toward the softer side.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Give Up the Funk Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f2ad1ba84fb8f045799"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"We Are The World - Co-Composer and Inception","body":"Lionel Richie co-composed \"We Are The World\" with Michael Jackson. The idea for the song, intended to support campaigns against the famines of the 1980s, was conceived at the Motown house. \"la nera del Richie y Michael Jackson (...) deciden instruir la canción un poco para las campañas que se hacían a favor de las grandísimas ranbronas que hubo de la otra 80.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"We are the world","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XTJKpU0lhls"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Academy Award for Best Original Song (1982) — Endless Love","body":"Nominated.","year":1982,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q112243"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Album of the Year (1984) — Can't Slow Down","body":"Nominated.","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q904528"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Song of the Year (1984) — Hello","body":"Nominated.","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1027904"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for International Album of the Year (1984) — Can't Slow Down","body":"Nominated.","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6314083"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"American Music Award for Favorite Pop/Rock Album (1985) — Can't Slow Down","body":"Nominated.","year":1985,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q19858080"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"American Music Award for Favorite Soul